## Project-specific context (mock3)

This project has two sibling TypeScript codelines, each with its own vitest suite. Tests live in `test/*.test.ts` and import from `../src/`; both codelines use vitest's `describe`/`it`/`expect` style. The vitest test files shown in the injected evidence are your ONLY oracle for kill/survive judgements — a mutation is "killed" only if a test actually present in the evidence would fail under it.

The two tickets in scope define the critical paths. Prioritise mutations on these:

- MOCK3-1 (fare boundary at age 65, mock-a `src/fares.ts`): the rule is "65 AND OVER pay the concession fare", so the correct check is `rider.age >= 65`. The current code uses `rider.age > 65`, which is itself a live off-by-one. Propose boundary-shift and comparison-inversion mutations here (`> 65` ↔ `>= 65`, `< 65`, boundary constants 64/65/66) and check them against `test/fares.test.ts`. Note the existing suite tests age 70 but NOT age 65 exactly — a mutation at the exact boundary may survive; if so, report it as survived with a recommendation to add a boundary test at age 65 (and 64). Also consider the `age < 13` child boundary and the `hasPass` early return (boolean negation / removed early return).
- MOCK3-2 (final stop omitted from schedule, mock-b `src/schedule.ts`): `formatStops` loops `i < stops.length - 1`, which drops the last stop. Propose loop-bound and index mutations (`length - 1` ↔ `length`, `length - 2`, `i <= ...`) and check them against `test/schedule.test.ts`. The existing tests only assert `toContain('->')` and `toContain('Union')` — they never assert the final stop ('Eglinton') appears, so a mutation that omits the last stop will likely SURVIVE. Report that as survived with a recommendation to assert the final stop is rendered. `totalMinutes` reads `stops[stops.length - 1]` — index-shift mutations there are worth checking too.

A mutation surviving in one codeline but killed in the other is a signal worth calling out in the recommendation field.

